  "nameStory": "The Golden Pholiota gets its shimmering name from the beautiful golden-yellow colors that dance across its cap, especially when young and fresh. The word 'pholiota' comes from the Greek word for 'scaly,' referring to the distinctive scales that often decorate these mushrooms' caps and stems.",
  "description": "Golden Pholiota is a medium-sized mushroom that grows in clusters on dead or dying hardwood trees. It displays stunning golden-yellow to orange-brown colors on its cap, which can range from 2-6 inches across. The mushroom has gills underneath its cap and typically grows in overlapping groups that can make dead logs look like they're sprouting golden flowers. Like other members of the scalycap family, it helps decompose dead wood in our forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Golden-yellow to orange-brown, 2-6 inches wide, often with small scales or patches on the surface that feel slightly rough to the touch. The cap may be sticky when wet and has a subtle honey-like sheen.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Start pale yellow and gradually turn rusty brown as spores mature. Gills are attached to the stem and feel somewhat crowded together when you look underneath the cap.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Sturdy and fibrous, often curved since it grows from vertical surfaces. May have a ring around the upper portion and small scales below the ring that match the cap color.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Rusty brown to dark brown when you place the cap gill-side down on white paper overnight. This brown dust is a key identifying feature.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Pleasant, slightly sweet or honey-like odor, especially noticeable when you break open the stem or crush a piece of the cap.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Golden Pholiota on dead or dying hardwood trees throughout New England's forests, particularly on birch, maple, and beech. They prefer moist woodland areas and can often be found near streams or in shaded valleys where the humidity stays high. You'll typically spot them growing in clusters on fallen logs or standing dead trees.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Rarely fruits in spring, though you might find dried specimens from the previous year still clinging to logs.",
    "summer": "Begins to appear in late summer, especially after periods of warm rain when humidity is high and consistent.",
    "fall": "Peak fruiting season when fresh, vibrant specimens are most common. Look for them from September through November, particularly after autumn rains.",
    "winter": "Fruiting stops, but dried, faded specimens may persist on logs throughout winter, appearing papery and brown."
  },
  "funFact": "Golden Pholiota can actually glow with a faint bioluminescence in complete darkness, though this green glow is so subtle that you need to let your eyes adjust in total darkness for several minutes to see it - making nighttime forest walks even more magical!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most commonly confused with Honey Mushrooms, which also grow in clusters on wood. However, Honey Mushrooms have white spore prints (not brown) and typically have a more prominent ring on the stem. Remember: 'Golden gives brown dust, Honey gives white powder.'",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite its appealing appearance and pleasant smell, Golden Pholiota's edibility is questionable and some people experience digestive upset. The 'golden' in its name specifically refers to the fresh, young specimens - older mushrooms often fade to duller browns and oranges.",
  "compareHints": "Golden Pholiota is smoother and less scaly than its relatives like Shaggy Scalycap, which is covered in prominent, shaggy scales. It's also larger and more golden than the smaller, more orange Flaming Scalycap. The pleasant honey-like smell helps distinguish it from many other scalycaps that have earthier or more pungent odors.",
